---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import moduleSource from "../../generated/examples/tutorial-modules-aha.kwe?raw";
import mainSource from "../../generated/examples/tutorial-modules-main.kwe?raw";

const aha = moduleSource.trimEnd();
const main = mainSource.trimEnd();
const output = `Ada
["Uche","Obi"]`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lesson 7: Modules"
  description="Learn Kwechiri named exports, imports, relative module paths, initialization order, and shared collection values."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Tutorial 07"
    title="Modules"
    lead="Split a program across two source files, export only the names another file needs, and use those names explicitly."
    meta="Beginner · Complete Lesson 6 first"
  />

  <div class="shell content-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Tutorial navigation">
      <a href="/tutorials/arrays-and-objects/">← Lesson 6: Arrays and Objects</a>
      <a href="/tutorials/modules/" aria-current="page">Lesson 7: Modules</a>
      <a href="/tutorials/standard-library-strings/">Lesson 8: Standard Library strings</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Two source files</h2>
      <p>
        A module is a <code>.kwe</code> source file. Create
        <code>aha.kwe</code> with a few declarations:
      </p>
      <CodeExample label="examples/tutorials/modules/aha.kwe" code={aha} />
      <p>
        The <code>bupụ</code> prefix exports a declaration. <code>nzuzo</code>
        has no prefix, so it remains private to this module.
      </p>

      <p>Now create <code>main.kwe</code> in the same directory:</p>
      <CodeExample
        label="examples/tutorials/modules/main.kwe"
        code={main}
        output={output}
      />

      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <p>From the repository root, run the entry file:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>node dist/cli/index.js examples/tutorials/modules/main.kwe</code></pre>
      <p>
        The compiler follows the import to <code>aha.kwe</code>, validates and
        initializes that dependency first, and then runs <code>main.kwe</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Exporting and importing</h2>
      <p>Only exported declarations can be imported:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>webata &#123; aha, kelee, ndepụta &#125; site na "./aha.kwe"</code></pre>
      <p>
        Each imported name is explicit. Imports must appear before all other
        statements. Trying to import <code>nzuzo</code> is an error because it
        was not exported.
      </p>
      <p>
        Module paths are relative and end in a lowercase <code>.kwe</code>
        extension. Absolute paths, bare package names, remote paths, directory
        imports, and extensionless paths are currently invalid. A module path
        resolves from the file that contains the import.
      </p>

      <h2>Initialization and cycles</h2>
      <p>
        A module initializes once, after its dependencies. If two files import
        each other directly or through a longer chain, the program is rejected
        as a module cycle instead of continuing with partially initialized
        values.
      </p>

      <h2>Read-only bindings, shared contents</h2>
      <p>An imported name cannot be rebound:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>aha = "Obi" # error: imported binding is read-only</code></pre>
      <p>
        Collections are different: the binding cannot be replaced, but the
        collection's existing contents remain mutable. The lesson changes
        <code>ndepụta[1]</code> to <code>"Obi"</code>. Imports do not copy an
        array or object, so every reference to that collection observes the
        change.
      </p>
      <p>
        This does not make the collection deeply immutable. It also does not
        allow resizing or changing the collection's established element type.
        Ordinary collection bounds, types, and mutation rules still apply.
      </p>

      <h2>Your turn</h2>
      <p>
        Change <code>aha</code> in <code>aha.kwe</code> to another string and
        predict the output. Then try importing <code>nzuzo</code> and explain
        why the module loader rejects it.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout--gold">
        <strong>Common problems</strong>
        Keep imports first and use the exact
        <code>webata &#123; ... &#125; site na "./file.kwe"</code> form. Use a
        relative lowercase <code>.kwe</code> path, import only exported names,
        and distinguish reassigning an imported binding from mutating an
        imported collection's contents.
      </div>

      <h2>Normative references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/modules.md`}>Modules</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/architecture/modules.md`}>Module architecture</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/design-decisions/ADR-019-module-semantics.md`}>Hardened module semantics</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/errors.md`}>Current language errors</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>
        Continue with
        <a href="/tutorials/standard-library-strings/">Lesson 8: Standard Library strings</a>
        and learn the five certified functions in <code>kwechiri:eriri</code>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
